WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.230 align:start position:0%
 
what is  this  oh yeah it's another average disney plus  reboot of the classic storyteller by the  dozen which was based on the 2003 steve  martin film which was based on the  original 1950 film which was based on  the 1948 novel also titled cheaper by  the dozen this retelling of the baker  family story starring zach braff and  gabrielle union has very little to do  with the other stories though in fact  the previous stories all had 12 kids  while this story has 10 kids and two  parents so it's not quite the same dozen  as you might think disney has an odd  knack of grabbing any low-hanging fruit  that's sure to make them some easy money  especially when it comes to sequels and  reboots typically they don't take as  much time or effort to produce and  they're usually able to slap an easily  recognizable name on the poster so they  get lots of viewers 2022's cheaper by  the dozen is no different for the most  part and pretty much follows the new  disney formula for reboots of classic  films while also trying to touch on a  bit of social commentary in a kid's  movie anyways we'll get to that later  for now let's talk about the opening  scene  the first scene introduces us to zoe and  paul baker two former divorcees who met  at paul's breakfast restaurant they  eventually got married and brought kids  from their previous marriages together  into one home along with two sets of  twins they had together now i know what  you're probably thinking this sounds  more like the movie yours mine and ours  than cheaper by the dozen and i'd  probably agree with you if the movie was  made in 2006 i think disney was simply  trying to show that mixed families are a  normal thing and not something people  should be ashamed of so it's nice to see  a different take on what a family can  look like other than the typical nuclear  family we're also introduced to each of  the baker children and gets some not so  necessary narration on their hopes and  dreams movies like this often fall into  the trap of telling us things to  introduce the story rather than showing  us things which is much more  entertaining to watch anyways the scene  also introduces us to kate paul's  ex-wife who is probably one of the worst  babysitters of all time what are you  guys playing drag race oh fun i love  rupaul she does however help zoe and  paul get all the kids out of the house  on time for the day we're also  introduced to the family's two dogs bark  obama and joe biden and i mean come on  that's kind of funny the movie does have  a couple of funny one-liners too  that's my daughter yes that's also my  daughter biologically she's mine yeah i  claim her on my taxes okay this line  actually brings me to my next point one  of the movie's main focuses is on  insecurity specifically paul's which is  only heightened when zoe's ex-husband  dom comes back into the picture paul's  threatened by dom and tries to do  everything he can to be successful to  give his kids everything they want but  eventually goes too far focusing only on  his work rather than on making his kids  and his family happy his insecurity  comes to its height at halftime during  their daughter's basketball game where  paul and dom face off in a dance battle  which is probably one of the most  unrealistic things about the entire  movie i mean what high school willingly  cuts their dance team's number short so  the crowd can watch two random guys  dance pretty poorly anyways next we find  out that paul has been asked to present  his special sauce to a group of  investors since it can be either hot  sweet or savory depending upon what you  put it on the investors are impressed  and provide them with seed money to  begin selling his sauce in grocery  stores this presentation is what  eventually leads paul down the rabbit  hole to his family's unhappiness he  uproots them from their humble cottage  in echo park and moves everyone to  calabasas then there's a few scenes  which are just there for feel-good  family movie filler and they show how  the big family just lives as a big  family which i can appreciate coming  from a large mix family myself as  they're unpacking though a security  guard informs them of a noise ordinance  for the neighborhood naturally he goes  straight to zoe who's the lone black  woman in the neighborhood it's at this  point when you start to understand one  of the main messages the film is trying  to get across kenya barris jennifer  reichskenzik and gail lerner all three  of whom worked on the hit series  blackish managed to sprinkle in some  lessons about the complexities of racial  identity in the film within the typical  messages about family understanding and  forgiveness for instance zoe instructs  her kids not to bring their toy guns to  the pool for fear that they might be  treated differently simply because of  the color of their skin and it's  understood that this is absolutely not  the way that people should have to live  their lives the writers did a pretty  good job of intermingling this social  commentary within the plot of the movie  and while some critics may say it was an  unnecessary addition to the film i think  it added something which parents or  other adults might appreciate in an  otherwise boring family film and it's  something that many people probably  needed to hear after selling the special  sauce for a while paul gets an  opportunity to franchise his restaurant  while zoe is hesitant she supports his  dreams but begins to struggle to keep up  with the kids haresh is getting into  fights deja is missing basketball  practice dj is struggling with how to  talk to girls and seth is struggling  with just fitting in with the family  this is when the story starts to get  pretty predictable even if you haven't  seen it yet you can probably bet that  we're about to enter what i call the pit  of the movie the pit is basically the  low point in predictable family movies  or rom-coms where the main characters go  through a breakup or a big fight that  eventually leads to forgiveness where  they all live happily ever after and  that's exactly what happens in cheaper  by the dozen paul says he has to stay  away for business for an extra week  meaning he would miss dj's birthday  party zoe hits a low point in taking  care of the kids all by herself and they  have a huge fight also it seemed as if  paul wasn't going to make his flight  back home in time so he would miss the  party entirely but as you might have  guessed paul was able to get back home  just in time quit the franchise business  move his family back to echo park and  live happily ever after although it was  a pretty predictable third act it still  left me feeling good about the movie's  outcomes perhaps that's why films like  this are so predictable so we feel a  level of comfort in knowing that it's  almost guaranteed that everything is  going to be okay it gives us a sense of  escapism because we know everything will  turn out alright while still taking  place in a somewhat realistic world  overall the new cheaper by the dozen  doesn't really make sense to anyone  who's coming in thinking it's going to  be a similar story to the steve martin  films and a lot of the themes in the  movie might not make sense to the target  audience kids but to parents or other  adults watching i think the movie might  have a little bit more to say than just  a typical family feel good movie all in  all it's a not so adventurous pretty  average disney movie worth a watch if  it's something you're interested in but  not necessarily something i'd say you  should go out of your way for anyways  that's going to be all for today if you  like what you saw please check out the  other video on your screen for some more  great content also don't forget to like  and subscribe as it truly does help me  out and thanks for watching rocket riley  you

